    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-b05896830f36::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-b05896830f36",
          "text": "“Nigeria’s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew by 2.55%(year-on-year) in real terms in the fourth quarter of 2019. Compared to the fourth quarter of 2018 which recorded a growth rate of 2.38%, this represents an increase of 0.17% points and an increase of 0.27% points when compared with the third quarter of 2019.”\n",
          "start": 430,
          "end": 744,
          "context": "According to NBS, the overall GDP in 2019 indicated a real growth of 2.27%, compared to 1.91% in 2018 while the real GDP grew by 5.59% quarter on quarter.\n “Nigeria’s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew by 2.55%(year-on-year) in real terms in the fourth quarter of 2019. Compared to the fourth quarter of 2018 which recorded a growth rate of 2.38%, this represents an increase of 0.17% points and an increase of 0.27% points when compared with the third quarter of 2019.”\n The report indicated that the growth represents the highest since the 2016 recession.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-eb0b849b5874::sentence-5",
          "documentId": "doc-eb0b849b5874",
          "text": "As with most other economies around the world, the sharp drop in Nigeria’s GDP growth is largely down to the slowdown in economic activity after the country resorted to a lockdown back in April to curb the spread of the virus. ",
          "start": 377,
          "end": 604,
          "context": "The -6.1% decline is also Nigeria’s steepest in the last 10 years.\n As with most other economies around the world, the sharp drop in Nigeria’s GDP growth is largely down to the slowdown in economic activity after the country resorted to a lockdown back in April to curb the spread of the virus.  In the wake of the pandemic the World Bank forecast a decline of -3.2% for 2020—a five percentage point drop from its previous projections.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-b05896830f36::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-b05896830f36",
          "text": "Nigeria’s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew by 2.55%(year-on-year) in real terms in the fourth quarter of 2019, according to a recently released report by the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS).\n",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 196,
          "context": "Nigeria’s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew by 2.55%(year-on-year) in real terms in the fourth quarter of 2019, according to a recently released report by the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS).\n The report surpassed the International Monetary Fund (IMF) projection of 2.1%.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-eb0b849b5874::sentence-13",
          "documentId": "doc-eb0b849b5874",
          "text": "The latest economic data shows Nigeria’s government continues to fall far short of projections in its Economic Recovery and Growth Plan, created in the aftermath of the 2016 recession to set out aggressive growth targets from 2017 to 2020. ",
          "start": 1706,
          "end": 1946,
          "context": "In fact, in the second quarter of 2020, local oil production dropped to its lowest since 2016—when Nigerian endured a full year of negative growth.\n The latest economic data shows Nigeria’s government continues to fall far short of projections in its Economic Recovery and Growth Plan, created in the aftermath of the 2016 recession to set out aggressive growth targets from 2017 to 2020.  There’s also little sign of a quick turnaround in Nigeria’s economic woes as the World Bank predicts Africa’s most populous country is set for its worst recession in four decades.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-eb0b849b5874::sentence-12",
          "documentId": "doc-eb0b849b5874",
          "text": "In fact, in the second quarter of 2020, local oil production dropped to its lowest since 2016—when Nigerian endured a full year of negative growth.\n",
          "start": 1558,
          "end": 1706,
          "context": "For context, the United States slashed its Nigerian crude oil imports oil by 11.67 million barrels in the first five months of 2020, compared to what it bought in the same period of 2019.  In fact, in the second quarter of 2020, local oil production dropped to its lowest since 2016—when Nigerian endured a full year of negative growth.\n The latest economic data shows Nigeria’s government continues to fall far short of projections in its Economic Recovery and Growth Plan, created in the aftermath of the 2016 recession to set out aggressive growth targets from 2017 to 2020. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-b05896830f36::sentence-6",
          "documentId": "doc-b05896830f36",
          "text": "“In Q4 2019, aggregate GDP stood at N39,577,340.04 million in nominal terms. This was higher than the fourth quarter of 2018 which recorded an aggregate of N35,230,607.63 million, representing year on year nominal growth rate of 12.34%.”\n",
          "start": 830,
          "end": 1068,
          "context": "The report indicated that the growth represents the highest since the 2016 recession.\n “In Q4 2019, aggregate GDP stood at N39,577,340.04 million in nominal terms. This was higher than the fourth quarter of 2018 which recorded an aggregate of N35,230,607.63 million, representing year on year nominal growth rate of 12.34%.”\n – Oil Sector –\n"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
