          "start": 430,
          "end": 744,
          "context": "According to NBS, the overall GDP in 2019 indicated a real growth of 2.27%, compared to 1.91% in 2018 while the real GDP grew by 5.59% quarter on quarter.\n “Nigeria’s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew by 2.55%(year-on-year) in real terms in the fourth quarter of 2019. Compared to the fourth quarter of 2018 which recorded a growth rate of 2.38%, this represents an increase of 0.17% points and an increase of 0.27% points when compared with the third quarter of 2019.”\n The report indicated that the growth represents the highest since the 2016 recession.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-b05896830f36::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-b05896830f36",
          "text": "Nigeria’s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew by 2.55%(year-on-year) in real terms in the fourth quarter of 2019, according to a recently released report by the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS).\n",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 196,
          "context": "Nigeria’s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew by 2.55%(year-on-year) in real terms in the fourth quarter of 2019, according to a recently released report by the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS).\n The report surpassed the International Monetary Fund (IMF) projection of 2.1%.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-eb0b849b5874::sentence-13",
          "documentId": "doc-eb0b849b5874",
          "text": "The latest economic data shows Nigeria’s government continues to fall far short of projections in its Economic Recovery and Growth Plan, created in the aftermath of the 2016 recession to set out aggressive growth targets from 2017 to 2020. ",
          "start": 1706,
          "end": 1946,
          "context": "In fact, in the second quarter of 2020, local oil production dropped to its lowest since 2016—when Nigerian endured a full year of negative growth.\n The latest economic data shows Nigeria’s government continues to fall far short of projections in its Economic Recovery and Growth Plan, created in the aftermath of the 2016 recession to set out aggressive growth targets from 2017 to 2020.  There’s also little sign of a quick turnaround in Nigeria’s economic woes as the World Bank predicts Africa’s most populous country is set for its worst recession in four decades.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-b05896830f36::sentence-3",
          "documentId": "doc-b05896830f36",
          "text": "According to NBS, the overall GDP in 2019 indicated a real growth of 2.27%, compared to 1.91% in 2018 while the real GDP grew by 5.59% quarter on quarter.\n",
          "start": 275,
          "end": 430,
          "context": "The report surpassed the International Monetary Fund (IMF) projection of 2.1%.\n According to NBS, the overall GDP in 2019 indicated a real growth of 2.27%, compared to 1.91% in 2018 while the real GDP grew by 5.59% quarter on quarter.\n “Nigeria’s Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew by 2.55%(year-on-year) in real terms in the fourth quarter of 2019. Compared to the fourth quarter of 2018 which recorded a growth rate of 2.38%, this represents an increase of 0.17% points and an increase of 0.27% points when compared with the third quarter of 2019.”\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-eb0b849b5874::sentence-11",
          "documentId": "doc-eb0b849b5874",
          "text": "For context, the United States slashed its Nigerian crude oil imports oil by 11.67 million barrels in the first five months of 2020, compared to what it bought in the same period of 2019. ",
          "start": 1370,
          "end": 1558,
          "context": "The accompanying steep drop in oil prices amid a drop in global demand left Nigeria drastically shorn of earnings given its dependence on the commodity as its biggest revenue source.  For context, the United States slashed its Nigerian crude oil imports oil by 11.67 million barrels in the first five months of 2020, compared to what it bought in the same period of 2019.  In fact, in the second quarter of 2020, local oil production dropped to its lowest since 2016—when Nigerian endured a full year of negative growth.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-eb0b849b5874::sentence-2",
          "documentId": "doc-eb0b849b5874",
          "text": "Nigeria’s economy contracted by 6.1% year on year in the second quarter of this year, latest reports from Nigeria’s statistics bureau show. ",
          "start": 102,
          "end": 242,
          "context": "The effects the coronavirus pandemic has had on Africa’s largest economy is starting to show in data.\n Nigeria’s economy contracted by 6.1% year on year in the second quarter of this year, latest reports from Nigeria’s statistics bureau show.  The dip follows thirteen quarters of positive but low growth rates. "
        }
      ]
    }
